# Authored, fact-bounded elaborations for the same continuous examples.
EXTRA={
0: "Follow the running example through both actions. With hot reload, ask what the screen should show given that its current application state is preserved. With hot restart, ask what should show after application initialization runs again and the earlier state is lost. Those are different expected results even when both actions use the same edited source. This comparison explains why you should choose an action from the changed code's need to execute again, rather than assuming that the most convenient action proves the edit. The final evidence is the intended change in the running app, not just an action taken in the terminal.",
1: "Trace the count declaration as a contract that continues after its first line. Its initializer establishes the inferred type, and its later assignment must remain within that type. Trace the label differently: the final modifier permits its one assignment but not a later replacement of that variable's value. For a const declaration, additionally ask whether the value is a compile-time constant. These questions let you explain the modifier choice in the supplied function: the count needs reassignment, the label does not, and a compile-time value has the additional const property. The declaration and the function's later statements should tell the same story.",
2: "The loop has two reports, with the source edit between them. The first report tells you what problem to investigate and where; the second tells you whether your documented correction addressed it. Keep those roles separate. For the supplied reassignment diagnostic, the useful reasoning is the connection between the message, the indicated statement, and the documented correction—not the number of lines changed. If reanalysis still reports the problem, the same three pieces of evidence remain the starting point. A clean result for that diagnostic closes this particular correction loop without requiring unrelated changes elsewhere.",
3: "Separate the eligibility expression into questions in the same order as its intended grouping. The amount comparison asks whether the amount meets the limit. The delivery boolean supplies another true-or-false input. Conjunction requires both; disjunction represents an alternative; negation reverses a boolean condition. Before combining them, write the intended decision in plain language so an alternative is not accidentally treated as a requirement. Parentheses then record the grouping you chose. The numerical division examples are a reminder that an operator expresses a specific operation, not just a general instruction to calculate something with two values.",
5: "The declaration is the caller's binding contract. Look at the parameter section before following the body: positional parameters answer which argument comes first or next, named parameters answer which label identifies the argument, and defaults answer what is used if an optional argument is omitted. Then follow the bound values to the returned result. A wrong result may come from binding the right values to the wrong positional parameters rather than from arithmetic inside the function. In the named case, check the parameter name and required marker independently. These checks keep calling rules separate from the computation they feed.",
6: "Think of the constructor as the transition from supplied arguments to an initialized instance. In the Order example, pair each argument with the final field it establishes and then read that field through the created instance. If the initializing formals are named, use their names for the pairing; if positional, use their positions. A final field's once-assigned rule remains in effect after construction. The important difference from an ordinary changing local variable is that the instance field cannot later be reassigned. Keep every required field's initialization visible rather than expecting the constructor body to repair a missing final-field initialization afterward.",
7: "Read the composition from both sides of each connection. The outer widget API says whether it accepts one child or a children list; the nested configuration supplies the widget or widgets that fill that role. This is why you should not infer the argument name solely from how many visual parts you see. In the changed-heading case, the old configuration still describes its old input and the new configuration describes the changed one. That separates immutable descriptions from the ordinary fact that an interface can have different descriptions over time. First check structure, then compare the input values in each description.",
8: "Repeatability is the important property to check in this build method. If the configuration says Ready, the returned subtree should describe Ready. If a later configuration says Dispatched, derive the description from that later input rather than assuming the earlier build was the only one. The framework can also call build because an inherited dependency changed. Treat the supplied context as part of the input relationship, not merely a parameter that is always irrelevant. By keeping the method free of side effects beyond widget building, you avoid assigning a one-time operation to a method the framework may call repeatedly.",
9: "Keep four measurements separate: the parent's minimum, the parent's maximum, the child's requested size, and the child's chosen size. The constraint interval determines which chosen sizes are permitted. A requested size outside that interval does not become valid just because the child wants it. The ConstrainedBox adds bounds within that relationship; it does not remove the parent's limits. This makes the two supplied parent cases genuinely different: a parent permitting the example's range allows the 150 limit, while tight parent constraints can force a size outside the added requested range. Follow the real incoming constraints before interpreting the bound.",
13: "The same control can be described in three useful ways: the visible area you select, its live widget-tree node, and the application source reached through the node's link. The Inspector joins those descriptions. Use the visible selection to avoid confusing similar source widgets, then use properties and layout information to explain the supplied symptom. Finally connect that explanation to the actual source location. None of these observations should be substituted for another: a source name alone does not establish which live widget you selected, and a screenshot alone does not expose the selected widget's properties or source link.",
14: "For an if/else-if chain, keep a record of which preceding conditions were false. That is what makes a later condition eligible to be tested. In the basic two-outcome example, true takes the if block and false takes the alternative. In a longer supplied chain, do not reason as though every condition is a separate independent if statement. The chain's ordering is part of its behavior. Once you have traced the matching branch, compare only its specified outcome with the input requirement. A bool condition is required at each test point, whether the bool comes from a supplied variable or an allowed comparison.",
15: "Follow the absent note without pretending it is a present string. The nullable type allows that absence; the chosen null-handling operation determines the next behavior. A check can establish presence for a local value where promotion applies, a fallback chooses another value for null, and conditional access avoids a member access when the receiver is null. An assertion instead claims that null is impossible at that point. These are different claims about the same input. In the supplied present and absent cases, state which claim is justified before choosing the operator, rather than treating all null-related operators as interchangeable conveniences.",
16: "Trace each collection using the relationship it promises. The list's first and second items have indexes 0 and 1, so an index is a position rather than an arbitrary key. The set's repeated tag is still a unique element, so it does not behave as a tally of every addition. The map associates a key with a value; a missing key can yield null even though stored values have a non-nullable declared type. In each case, the type arguments constrain what can be inserted. Keep those relationship and type questions separate when checking which supplied operations are valid.",
17: "In the quantity-total example, the accumulator after one iteration becomes the starting result for the next. Write that connection explicitly: start at 0, contribute 2, carry 2 forward, contribute 3, carry 5 forward, then contribute 1. The final 6 depends on retaining the earlier contributions. If a supplied rule uses continue, record what contribution or later body statement is skipped on that iteration. If it uses break, record that there are no later iterations. For a conventional loop, also record the update after each completed iteration so the condition's next evaluation uses the intended value.",
18: "A callback has both a definition site and an invocation site. At definition, its body can refer to variables from its enclosing lexical scope. At passing, the receiving API gets the function value. At invocation, the body uses that retained scope access. Keeping those three moments visible explains why an immediate function call is a different argument expression: it runs the body instead of handing over the function value for the receiver to invoke. The anonymous block and arrow forms are alternate ways to write the callback, not promises about asynchronous timing or copied snapshots of captured variables.",
19: "In the type-and-key comparison, mark configuration as a separate concern. A label change updates what the description says, while equal runtimeType and key allow the existing element to receive that new description. If the type or key changes, the stated update condition no longer holds even if the label looks identical. This prevents reasoning backward from a visual similarity to element identity. Use the old/new pair supplied in the exercise and explain which part of the identity condition stays equal and which changes. Do not replace the condition with a general impression that the widgets look like the same control.",
20: "Use the State instance as the owner-lifetime line through the example. Several builds can happen along that line without starting a new State lifetime. Initialization belongs at the start of that ownership; a configuration-dependent replacement belongs at the update that changes the resource; disposal belongs at permanent removal. The resource's binding can change while the State continues to exist. This is why a build count is not a reliable resource-creation count. For the supplied replacement, identify the old binding that must be detached and the new one that must be established, then identify the remaining resource to release at the end.",
21: "The supplied count has two observations: its stored field value and the UI derived from it. The setState callback changes the first synchronously, and the scheduled build reflects that change in the second. This distinction explains why merely observing a changed field is not the whole UI verification. Likewise, scheduling a build without making the intended field change would not establish the specified new count. Keep the callback itself synchronous and limited to the local mutation. The completion of an asynchronous operation, when later taught, can be outside that callback rather than changing this synchronous contract.",
22: "The extracted widget has an input boundary and an action boundary. Final constructor values describe the differences between the two instances, while the callback says which action the instance delegates. Follow the original label into the extracted input and the original delegated action into the callback. Those two traces provide a direct preservation check without relying on the fact that the code now has fewer repeated lines. A dedicated widget is the requested extraction unit. Keep its responsibility the same size as the repeated section, and keep caller-specific work at the caller rather than hiding it in the shared child.",
23: "Follow the invalid input all the way to the action boundary. Its validator returns an error string; validate combines the field results and returns false if a field reports an error; the submission branch must therefore not execute. Follow the corrected input through the same path: validators return null, the overall result is true when no field reports an error, and the one handler invocation enters its single submission branch. This ordering explains both checks the exercise requires: invalid input does not submit, and valid handling does not duplicate submission within the same invocation. The stable key provides access to the intended Form state.",
24: "Treat allocated space and chosen child size as two measurements. The 1:2 factors divide the illustrative 300 units into 100 and 200 allocated units; Expanded then requires filling the assigned share, while loose Flexible permits a smaller child. A proportion alone does not describe that fit decision. Also, the 300-unit value is remaining main-axis space, not a claim about every device or the entire screen width. Before using the ratio, establish that this remaining space is finite under the parent's constraints. If that starting point is unbounded, the proportional arithmetic cannot supply the missing finite allocation.",
26: "The list's content and its viewport answer different size questions. The content can include more orders than are visible; the viewport is the bounded area through which the user reaches them. ListView.builder describes how dynamic children are built by index on demand. Its itemCount must describe the supplied collection, and each builder index must address the intended item. When the list is placed in a Column, the bounded-parent condition remains important for the Expanded arrangement in the brief. Check that relationship before interpreting the ability to scroll as proof the surrounding layout has the required viewport bounds.",
27: "The narrow and wide traces should use the width named by the requirement. If the subtree has 500 available units inside a larger window, the local decision uses 500, not the window's width. LayoutBuilder supplies the local constraints for that question; MediaQuery.sizeOf supplies the app window's size for a different question. Once the measurement is chosen, the comparison establishes which layout branch to return. Keep the equality case visible in the supplied breakpoint rule. A test that happens to make local and window widths equal cannot by itself demonstrate that the correct source was chosen for a locally constrained section.",
28: "Use the same content in the ordinary and enlarged-text checks. The changing input is the operating system font-size setting, which changes the space text requires. If required content overflows or becomes inaccessible, the layout needs adjustment for that larger requirement. The bounded Row and Flexible pattern addresses the supplied width problem without claiming every layout is solved by one wrapper. The small-target, largest-setting check is valuable because it tests the required content under the specified demanding combination, not just a comfortable default-size layout. Record what remains accessible after the change rather than judging only whether the screen looks tidy.",
30: "Draw one outline for the decoration and another for the interactive control. The recommendation applies to the latter. Compare the Android target with its dp recommendation and the iOS target with its points recommendation without silently treating the units as one universal value. In the ConstrainedBox pattern, requiredWidth and requiredHeight stand for the layout minimums chosen for the relevant control and target. The example does not require enlarging the picture to those dimensions. Its central check is whether the interactive area has sufficient space independently of the artwork that identifies the action.",
31: "Use the same A and B identities in both list orders. Before reordering, each identity produces its own key. After reordering, the positions differ but those identity-derived keys still name the same items. The framework's match also includes the same widget type. This is why the comparison table needs identity, position, key, and type rather than only index and label. If a key is made from the new position instead, it names that position rather than preserving the item's identity through the move. Check the stateful row result as well as the key values under the supplied reorder fixture.",
32: "Read the shared-ancestor diagram as an ownership map. The summary and action panel are consumers in separate branches, so the owner must be above both. Configuration gives each consumer the current state it needs, while callbacks give children a way to request the owner's change. That division avoids pretending the child owns the same state just because it can request a mutation. In the supplied tree, use the ancestor relation to explain why the chosen owner reaches both branches. Do not select a separate duplicate state owner in each branch when the feature requires one shared value.",
33: "The notification announces a model change that has already been made. In the count example, put the update to 3 before notifyListeners so a notified read sees the exposed new count. Fields or getters provide that read contract. A mere getter read is not a mutation and is not a reason to notify. The condition for notification is that a model change might change consumer UI; this retains the brief's qualification rather than requiring notification on every access. Trace the mutation and notification separately, then show the value consumers can read at the moment they receive the notification.",
34: "Provider scope is determined at the lookup context, not by the visual title of the whole screen. Start at that context and trace its ancestors for the requested type. The nearest matching provider is the value the lookup resolves. The new provider cannot serve a context above itself, even if the source code for the two is nearby. In the supplied existing-model example, Provider.value exposes that instance to the child subtree. Check both the placement of the value and the placement of each consumer's context. A missing matching non-nullable provider is a throwing lookup, not an automatically supplied empty model.",
36: "Separate what the widget displays from the rest of the model. A full-model dependency can be expressed with watch, while select names the part needed for the output. Consumer gives its builder the provided model and rebuilds that consumer on model notification. These are subscription choices, unlike a non-listening action read. Then separate dependency choice from placement: put the subscription near the UI that uses it rather than around unrelated content. In the badge example, describe the badge's rendered value first, then explain which selected or watched model data supplies it and where that relationship lives in the tree.",
37: "Begin with the model's origin. If create constructs it for this ChangeNotifierProvider, the provider owns that new model and disposes it when leaving the tree. If the supplied model already has an owner, value exposes that existing model. The constructor choice communicates a lifetime relationship rather than an incidental syntax preference. In the ownership diagram, keep the owner of the existing model visible instead of assuming that exposing it transfers creation responsibility. Use that distinction when comparing the two snippets, and do not combine them into a single pattern that treats every passed model as newly provider-owned.",
38: "Follow one interaction through the supplied boundaries. The view forwards its input to the command; application logic orchestrates the action and exposes the state the view renders; source-specific work stays in the data layer. The refactor succeeds when these responsibility locations change as intended while the input and observable outcome remain the same. This is a recommended architectural division, so preserve that scope instead of calling it the only valid Flutter structure. The most useful before/after comparison is the same event and result, with the source operation no longer implemented by the view's event handler.",
39: "Compare the production screen's requirements with the harness tree, not with the entire production application. For each dependency the test screen needs, identify the ancestor or model value that the harness actually supplies. PumpWidget builds and renders that test tree; it does not implicitly borrow missing ancestors from another tree. The supplied Material screen therefore needs its relevant MaterialApp environment in the harness, and its lookups need models placed above it. Keep the initial rendering check focused on this construction. Later finder and interaction assertions will have a meaningful screen to inspect because this environment is explicit.",
40: "The test assertion has three components: a built tree, a finder targeting the requirement, and a match-count expectation. In the Ready-label case, text defines the target and findsOneWidget defines the acceptable count. A key or type target uses its corresponding finder instead. FindsNothing is a deliberate absence expectation, not simply an assertion that something else exists. Trace these pieces in order so a test failure can be interpreted against the stated visible requirement. Do not treat the presence of any widget of a broad type as automatically proving a more specific label or keyed-control requirement.",
41: "Keep event simulation and output inspection on opposite sides of the pump. EnterText supplies the field input and tap supplies the action. Pump processes a frame before the changed UI is asserted. PumpAndSettle is useful for the finite scheduled-frame sequence specified by the example, not as an assumption that all possible work finishes after a tap. With the supplied invalid form input, the acceptance target is the resulting error UI after frame processing. This makes the test verify what the interaction produced rather than merely whether the test invoked the interaction method without an immediate problem.",
42: "The success trace and failure trace leave the try block differently. A normal result permits its dependent successful path; a throw unwinds toward a matching handler. At that handler, decide from the supplied boundary contract whether to produce a failure outcome or rethrow to another handler. The known exception type makes that decision specific. If the trace is needed, the second catch parameter provides it. Keep caught-failure handling distinct from success: suppressing a failure without the required translation or propagation does not establish the successful result that the operation would have returned.",
43: "Use the report's parts as a sequence of evidence. The exception says what was reported, the framework context describes where it was surfaced, and the stack helps locate the relevant application call. The first relevant application frame gives the source and variables to inspect. This is different from assuming the top framework reporting frame is the defective statement. In the supplied callback failure, connect the observed exception with the chosen application source so the report explains a concrete call rather than prompting a broad rewrite. Keep FlutterError.onError's reporting role distinct from the source of the application failure.",
44: "Draw three points even for the already-completed Future case: completion, then registration, and callback execution. The last point occurs in a later microtask when then is registered after completion. That ordering explains the otherwise confusing observation that the Future is already completed but the callback has not yet run. For a newly uncompleted Future, distinguish the waiting representation from the eventual value-or-error outcome as well. Future<T> identifies the successful result type without turning the Future object into a value of T. The supplied onValue and onError callbacks receive distinct outcomes; lack of callback execution alone does not identify the completion state.",
45: "Follow only statements that depend on the awaited value. Their order is constrained by that dependency: obtain the completed value before using it. While the function is suspended, other asynchronous work can run, so the trace is not an application-wide synchronous pause. In the failure fixture, the awaited operation routes its expected failure to the typed handler instead of producing a value for those dependent statements. Check the declared Future return type against what the function is intended to complete with. This separates the function's asynchronous contract from the immediate synchronous appearance of statements written after await.",
46: "Make a library-to-reference pairing for the supplied file. The URI identifies the imported library; its exported names become available according to show or hide selection; a prefix qualifies references when used. If two supplied libraries expose the same name, the prefix at the reference identifies the intended library. Check that the selected name has not been excluded by hide or omitted from show. An SDK dart: URI and a package: URI mark different library origins. This is enough to diagnose the supplied name-availability problem without changing dependency versions or searching for a different package.",
47: "Follow the supplied call through two separate contracts. The request contract specifies method, endpoint, headers, and any payload. The response contract specifies how statusCode and body become an application outcome. Await obtains the Response for that second check; it does not predetermine that outcome. The two recipes' success checks illustrate why GET and POST examples should not be flattened into one universal status test: 200 is accepted for the fetch recipe and 201 for the create recipe. Keep those qualifications in the example and use the exercise's actual endpoint contract when tracing its response.",
48: "The wire-to-model table should preserve both names and types. A decoded field under the contract's identity key must feed the intended model identity with its declared type; the model-to-wire mapping must return the prescribed key and JSON-compatible value. A mapping that keeps values but changes key spelling has not preserved the wire contract. Likewise, decoding alone leaves the untyped representation rather than completing the typed model construction. Trace the same supplied fields in both directions so you can point to their agreement, while using the existing JSON foundation rather than introducing a new general serialization format.",
49: "Keep pending, failed, empty-success, and non-empty-success traces apart. Pending says the result is not yet established. Failed says completion produced an error. Empty-success says completion succeeded and the collection contains no elements. Non-empty-success says usable data is present. Publishing one corresponding presentation at each transition makes those meanings visible. A FutureBuilder branch must check completion state and error presence before using its data, and the empty check belongs after successful completion. Use the same supplied request in each fixture so the comparison changes the outcome, not the meaning of the feature or its endpoint contract.",
50: "Compare two timelines with the same asynchronous operation. In the first, the State remains mounted through completion, so the post-await guard permits the intended update. In the second, disposal occurs before completion, so the guard returns before setState. Checking liveness at the start cannot substitute for checking it after the interval in which disposal could occur. Where the supplied source can be canceled or detached, perform that lifetime cleanup too. The mounted guard and possible cancellation serve related but distinct responsibilities: preventing a disposed-owner update and stopping or detaching owned work where that is possible.",
51: "Draw the timeout Future and the original operation as separate lines. The timeout line can complete with TimeoutException at the supplied limit without stopping the original line. A later original completion is therefore compatible with the earlier timeout result. Retry starts a new invocation of the request function and its presentation states, not a reinterpretation of the previous timeout as success. Check these distinct events in the exercise's delayed-operation fixture. The timeout bound answers how long the returned Future waits; it does not answer whether the source operation has been canceled or whether a retry is already underway.",
52: "Use the same A/B labels for both successful and failed completions. If B is current, A's captured identity mismatches whether A returns data or an error. The comparison therefore precedes either publication. If B's captured identity matches the current identity, its outcome belongs to the current request under this mechanism. Request identity is separate from owner liveness: an owner may still be alive while A is obsolete. FutureBuilder's callback-identity behavior is an example of ignoring callbacks after replacement or disposal, not a statement that asynchronous work always completes in the order it began.",
53: "Check the contract from the caller's perspective. The caller can use the instance members exposed through Contract, so Implementation must provide compatible APIs even though it does not inherit their implementation through implements. For an overriding method in the extends case, compare positional-parameter count, parameter types, return type, and generic status with the original method. Same-or-broader accepted parameter types and same-or-narrower return types preserve the stated compatibility. Mark the intentional override with @override. Keep those member comparisons explicit so a familiar method name is not mistaken for a complete interface or override compatibility check.",
54: "Follow the result back across the repository boundary. Source access and raw-data conversion occur inside the data layer; the application-facing method returns the application's contract. The view model consumes that contract without making the UI depend on the concrete transport or storage API. This is why moving only a line while leaving the raw source response in the UI does not demonstrate the intended boundary. In the supplied order request, identify where source representation stops and application data begins. Preserve the caller's intended result while hiding how that result was obtained from the external source.",
55: "The test has an operation-completion point and a UI-rendering point. Awaiting controlled work establishes the former; pumping after completion establishes the frame needed before checking the changed UI. Pump with a duration also advances the test clock by that duration. PumpAndSettle instead uses the absence of scheduled frames as its stopping condition. If ongoing activity keeps scheduling frames, use the supplied finite known completion point rather than confusing that activity with work guaranteed to settle. Record exactly which completed work makes the assertion meaningful and which subsequent pump renders its effect.",
56: "Read the stack snapshots as the same underlying journey. Before push, the list is the current top route. After push, the destination is current while the list remains beneath it. After pop, the destination is removed and the list route below is revealed. This makes return behavior a removal from the stack rather than a fresh push of a replacement list. Use the supplied route builder to describe the destination being pushed. Keep the top-route observation separate from the list of all stack entries, so seeing one screen does not lead you to assume there are no routes underneath it.",
58: "The outward and return parts of the route have different data directions. The caller pushes and awaits completion; the destination pops with the selected value; the caller checks the nullable result and owner liveness before using it to update UI. If the route is dismissed without selection, the null branch represents that absence rather than a model with invented default fields. If the caller was disposed during the await, a non-null result still cannot justify an owner update. Trace those conditions independently so a successful selection is not used as proof that both nullability and lifetime checks have been satisfied.",
59: "For each location, follow the configured path to its builder and screen configuration. MaterialApp.router connects the application to that GoRouter. The go call then replaces the stack with the screens configured for the location, which is a different operation from pushing one new route on top of any existing stack. Keep the location and resulting stack linked in the trace. Do not infer the result solely from the destination widget's name, because the configured location describes which screens the router uses for that navigation. Parameter handling and exceptions are separate details that this initial configuration trace does not need to invent.",
60: "The integer-route example has three useful gates: raw lookup, presence, and valid parse. An absent lookup stops before conversion. A present malformed string fails int.tryParse and must be rejected before the destination is constructed. A present valid string supplies the typed identity only after parsing succeeds. Keep the path map and query map distinct when doing the first lookup. This sequence prevents a nullable or malformed value from being treated as the destination's required integer simply because the URL reached the router. Use the same prescribed identity contract for all three supplied parameter cases.",
61: "Choose the configured recovery mechanism before predicting the failure screen. If errorBuilder is the applicable choice, it receives state.error and constructs the deliberate ErrorScreen. If onException is provided, it takes precedence over errorBuilder and errorPageBuilder and can choose recovery navigation. This is why the exercise should not promise that every configured builder will run on the same exception. Trace the unmatched or unusable location into its routing exception and then through the actual configured choice. Keep the recovery outcome distinct from the original requested screen so the app does not present a routing failure as successful destination resolution.",
62: "The external entry check joins two mappings: platform URL delivery and router path matching. The documented app-link or universal-link setup addresses the platform part; the configured GoRouter path addresses the in-app part. Verify both through the supplied URL fixtures. Cold-start and running-app entry are separate observations because initial platform navigation differs between Android and iOS. A successful result in one state should not be relabeled as evidence for the other. Record which entry state was tested and which destination appeared, while keeping platform configuration supplied rather than writing native handlers beyond the path's scope.",
63: "Use canPop as the policy input and didPop as the reported result, not as interchangeable booleans. In the callback, didPop true means the route already popped, so return. When the attempt is reported as not popped, follow the supplied confirmation policy before an allowed pop. Keep the iOS CupertinoRouteTransitionMixin qualification beside that trace: canPop false prevents gesture detection and no callback is invoked for that gesture. Android's blocked system-back attempt reports didPop false instead. The expected test evidence therefore differs by platform; absence of an iOS gesture callback is not an unreported Android-style successful dismissal.",
65: "Compare evidence in two groups. Capability and supported platforms answer whether the package can serve the feature on its targets. SDK/platform requirements answer whether it can fit this application configuration. Completeness, documentation and API usability, publisher, license, and quality score inform the broader evaluation. A Favorite designation does not remove either group of checks. Use the supplied candidates to mark exactly which requirement is met or unmet, rather than writing an unsupported general claim that one package is best. The selection outcome should follow the feature's needs and compatible target setup, not a badge alone.",
66: "Read constraints as allowed ranges and the lockfile as recorded resolution. Pub must resolve a mutually compatible dependency graph within the constraints. A caret range is broader than a single pinned version but still bounded by its breaking-version boundary. If the supplied conflict has no mutually supported range as written, adjust it using the supported compatibility information and resolve again. An override that forces an unsupported version does not supply the missing compatibility evidence. Keep the application's resolved lockfile so the chosen versions are recorded, while avoiding a promise that every future range adjustment or forced version is safe.",
67: "Use the same plugin with separate target-setup checks. The Dart dependency makes the package part of the project, but its documented Android or iOS installation requirements may still be needed. For permission_handler, compare AndroidX and the documented compile SDK with the supplied Android project. For iOS, identify whether the project uses Swift Package Manager or CocoaPods and follow the matching instructions. Build/run verification on each required platform closes that setup check. Keep documented version requirements and integration mode explicit rather than replacing them with an invented minimum number or assuming one platform's build proves the other's integration.",
69: "The permission trace has an intent, a status, a request when needed, and a result-dependent action. The supplied feature determines why authorization is relevant; the returned status determines whether that feature can proceed. Requesting an already-granted permission produces its status without displaying another authorization dialog. This prevents the example from teaching a dialog as the mandatory observable result of every request call. Keep the user's decision in the branch outcome, and keep the request limited to feature-relevant access. Use the contract's non-proceeding behavior when authorization is not granted rather than treating the request call itself as permission.",
70: "Keep the source of each status beside its meaning. A request result can identify permanentlyDenied, which calls for a settings change rather than another prompt. Android's status getter still reports denied for permanent denial, so a getter-only trace cannot establish that distinction. Restricted is an operating-system limitation, not the same description as a user denial. For denied or restricted access, the supplied unavailable-feature fallback is the outcome rather than the protected action. When settings is appropriate for permanent denial, offer it deliberately instead of running a repeating permission prompt sequence as though the status guaranteed another dialog.",
71: "The feature has at least two distinct questions in the permission-backed service example: is access authorized, and is the service enabled? Device/platform capability adds the supplied availability question where applicable. A granted status answers authorization, not all of those questions together. ServiceStatus.isEnabled is exposed for supported services; do not turn that into a universal check for every capability. Use the adapter's supplied availability result where that is the feature's contract. In the absent-capability or disabled-service fixture, choose the alternative behavior before invoking the unavailable feature rather than presenting a failed invocation as the normal availability test.",
75: "Explain each selection by the operations the feature needs. The display setting has a small simple typed value and is non-critical, matching preferences. The text document is read and written as file contents, matching an application file. The structured local collection fits SQLite's database role. These are contextual examples of the supplied mechanisms rather than an exhaustive taxonomy of all possible storage systems. Keep the preferences durability limitation beside the importance decision: an awaited setter does not guarantee durable persistence when it returns. If the proposed value is critical, do not let its small size alone justify preferences.",
79: "A schema-to-model comparison has names, types, and identity. The table name groups the rows; column names identify the stored fields; INTEGER PRIMARY KEY supplies the stated integer identity constraint. The model's values must use storage representations sqflite supports. For the boolean example, encode as 0/1 rather than treating the Dart bool as directly supported. The supplied creation callback creates tables for the initial database version at its path. Keep those placeholders visibly tied to the supplied schema, so copying a generic tableName or fieldName is not mistaken for implementing the application's actual model contract.",
80: "Follow creation in four stages: supplied model, supported column map, awaited insert, and returned row identity. Map conversion is not yet insertion, and insertion being started is not yet a successful creation outcome. For a successful ordinary insert, the integer return is the row identity the feature can retain for later addressing. If the later caller needs that identity, do not discard it merely because the visible label has already been displayed. Keep the qualification about ordinary successful insertion and keep values within the schema's supported types rather than assuming the helper converts every application field automatically.",
81: "The selectedColumns, identity predicate, and bound argument describe what the read asks for. The returned list describes what was actually found. An empty list must take the absence branch before a first-row access. A row map still needs conversion to the typed model using the prescribed names and types. This separates three possible mistakes: asking for the wrong identity, assuming a match exists, and mapping a returned field incorrectly. Use the supplied known and absent identities to check both list outcomes, while keeping the value in whereArgs rather than constructing predicate text by interpolation.",
82: "Compare target scope and changed fields separately. The predicate selects the intended record; valuesMap selects the fields being edited. The returned affected-row count tells whether a row matched, while the verification re-read proves the required field change and any required preservation. A matching count is therefore not a substitute for a field-by-field read when the exercise asks for that proof. Keep unrelated records outside the predicate and unrelated fields outside the intended change. Trace the supplied missing-record case as a no-match outcome rather than inventing an inserted replacement row that the update contract did not request.",
83: "The deletion proof has a removed side and a retained side. The selected identity should no longer be returned by a query, while other identities should still return their records. The affected-row count connects the operation with whether a row matched, but the follow-up reads establish the stated presence/absence outcomes. Keeping the identity predicate gives the action its scope; omitting it changes which records the operation can remove. Use the same fixture identities before and after deletion so the preservation check is meaningful, and do not equate a zero-match call with proof of removing a known existing record.",
84: "Migration must serve both installation histories. Fresh installation uses the updated onCreate path for the latest schema. An existing older-version database uses version increment and guarded onUpgrade steps. In the additive-change example, the new column is added while existing table records are retained. Reading records created under the old schema is therefore central evidence, not an optional substitute for creating empty tables. Match each step's guard to the old-version condition so the supplied upgrade history receives the intended change. Keep the fresh and upgraded fixtures separate rather than treating a successful empty-database creation as old-record preservation.",
85: "Use exception propagation to predict the transaction result. If the callback completes without an exception, it commits; if an exception escapes, it rolls back. Catching a required failure inside the callback and suppressing it changes that prediction, so propagation is part of the operation's correctness. Within the transaction, use txn for operations rather than the database object, which can deadlock in this pattern. At the application-facing boundary, expected DatabaseException handling turns a persistence failure into the prescribed failure result. Trace each operation's awaited completion so a success message cannot precede an unfinished or failed required write.",
86: "A repeated-event source has a listening relationship that outlasts one data callback. The returned StreamSubscription is the handle representing that listener's ownership. Data, error, and done callbacks describe event handling; cancellation describes ending the relationship. In the State-source replacement, stop the old listener before starting the new one and cancel the final listener at disposal. These are lifetime transitions, not additional data events. Keep the old and replacement sources labeled throughout the trace so canceling one is not mistaken for ending the other. The supplied owner determines when the subscription is no longer needed.",
87: "The client and server checks have different authorities. Client validation can explain to a user why an action is unavailable, but the remote policy must still protect the operation if that client check is bypassed. The supplied deny fixture tests that boundary. An allow-all test configuration permits anyone to read and overwrite data, so it cannot be relabeled as a protected production setup. Keep the intended access policy in force while evaluating permitted and denied operations. This is the security distinction the learner must carry into later service use; it does not require inventing backend code or new rule conditions here.",
88: "Startup ordering has a clear dependency: code using Firebase services comes after completed Firebase initialization. The binding initialization precedes that asynchronous startup, and the generated current-platform options provide the target configuration. Rebuild/run includes the configured platform integration. Trace those responsibilities without merging them: an options file supplies values, initializeApp performs initialization, await establishes completion before dependent service use, and rebuilding applies the configuration to the target integration. The supplied target should be the same throughout. Do not infer that a configuration belonging to another platform satisfies the current one simply because the Dart call has the same shape.",
89: "Read the sign-in action from invocation to completion. The supplied enabled email/password provider makes this the applicable API. Successful completion gives UserCredential, while FirebaseAuthException and its code supply the documented failure path. A tap starts the attempt but cannot determine which completion follows. For sign-out, await the operation and let the authentication-state owner reflect the result rather than using an invented independent session flag. Keep the action result and reflected session view distinguishable, so the example shows what this API establishes without pretending that button interaction alone is authoritative authentication state.",
90: "Use the same nullable-user interpretation for the initial and later events. The initial event supplies current sign-in state after subscription; subsequent events follow sign-in or sign-out changes. Null represents signed out and a User represents signed in in both cases. Waiting for that initial event avoids presuming a stored session before the stream establishes it. The subscription also has a lifetime owner and a cancellation point. Trace those responsibilities separately from the sign-in API's action result: the session listener is the ongoing source of the view's session state, not a one-time button completion.",
91: "The redirect table should contain both protected and allowed locations. A signed-out request for a protected route returns a permitted location under the supplied policy. The sign-in location remains allowed while signed out; a request already permitted returns null or its original path to continue. These cases prevent routing from becoming a repeated redirect to the same sign-in location. The resulting route controls presentation, while the backend still authorizes protected data. Keep that authority distinction beside the navigation table rather than implying that an app-side redirect can enforce the remote policy by itself.",
92: "The reference identifies the same document throughout the edit. A read obtains its snapshot and requires absence checks before model mapping. The payload maps the edited model into the contract's keys. Ordinary set creates or overwrites the addressed document; merge set is the supplied choice when unspecified fields must remain. Completion precedes the success outcome in either write case. This chain explains why preserving only the document ID is not enough if the collection path or encoded field keys change. Keep path, mapping, merge choice, and awaited completion visible when verifying the supplied partial edit.",
93: "The access-denial trace should retain the policy's meaning at every step. Rules evaluate the request, a denied operation produces the documented Firebase failure, the typed handler checks its code, and the client reports an access failure. None of those steps produces successful data or a successful mutation. Nor does client recovery include making rules allow all. Under the supplied policy, authorized access must meet permitted conditions. Keep the exception-code distinction tied to the documented outcome instead of broadly labeling every FirebaseException as a denial, and keep the denied-operation UI separate from an empty successful result.",
94: "The departing account, other accounts, and device-wide data have separate ownership categories under the retention policy. Identify those categories before deletion so the removable set is deliberate. Selected preference or secure keys are removed individually; database rows use the ownership predicate and bound arguments. Await those operations, then inspect both the selected old-owner data and the retained data. Checking only that some data disappeared would miss an accidental wipe of preserved records. The transition stream supplies the account change, but the retention policy supplies what should be removed; neither implies automatic transfer of old data to the next account.",
96: "Use the notification entry state to choose the API before interpreting the payload. A terminated-app opening uses the nullable initial-message result; a background-app opening uses the opened-app stream. Both carry destination data that must pass the route contract's validation. Foreground onMessage receipt is a different event and does not prove the user opened the notification. Trace valid, absent, and invalid destination data through the same contract, choosing only its intended navigation outcome. Keep subscription lifetime management for the owned repeated event path distinct from the one initial-message await.",
97: "Check consent and payload minimization independently. If required consent is absent, collection stays disabled and the code should not construct an unnecessary personal-data payload. With consent, use only the event contract's approved non-sensitive fields. Then verify the case-sensitive event name, prescribed parameter names, allowed name characters and length, and supported value types. These checks describe the supplied event rather than a reason to add more fields. The same repeated interaction must reuse its prescribed name and parameters. Collection toggling controls the consent-dependent state but does not justify passing an entire user or application object to logEvent.",
98: "The frame comparison needs an applicable refresh-rate budget and two work-stage observations. UI-thread work builds the layer tree, while raster-thread work rasterizes it. A roughly 16 ms frame budget corresponds to 60 Hz and a roughly 8 ms budget to 120 Hz. Compare the selected frame's stage timings with the relevant budget, then use timeline events to locate expensive work. Keep measurement in profile mode on the representative physical device so the evidence is not debug timing mislabeled as production behavior. Recording the supplied interaction before changing code makes the proposed investigation follow observed work rather than a generic guess.",
99: "Link cause and observed work rather than counting builds without context. One state change has its instrumented notification or setState evidence; Track Widget Builds shows named build events. Trace the watch dependency or ancestor invalidation connecting that change to the rebuilt section. Then compare what the section actually renders with what changed. If its rendered values are unrelated to that change, the trace identifies overly broad dependency work under the supplied example. Keep that relevance comparison explicit so a normal build driven by relevant visible data is not incorrectly called unnecessary just because a build event appears in the timeline.",
100: "The fix should match the traced source of broad work. If setState reaches too large a tree, localize it to the changing section. If a consumer depends too broadly on a model, select its needed part and preserve an unchanged subtree through Consumer.child as appropriate. Split widgets by how sections change and use const where possible. Then repeat the same measured interaction with the same visible outcome. This comparison is the evidence of reduced build work. A prettier widget split or a const keyword in source is not, by itself, the measured before/after result the exercise asks for.",
101: "Use reachability to explain the retained instance, not just a rising number. The retaining path shows which root-linked owner or callback reference still reaches the object. Baseline and repeated route-exit counts show persistent accumulation for the supplied resource; cleanup addresses the owning lifetime. Repeat the same route visits after disposing or canceling that owned resource and compare with the same baseline. This keeps diagnosis and verification connected. Leaving a route is an event in the journey, while becoming unreachable is a reference relationship; the retaining path explains why those are not automatically the same outcome.",
102: "Match each platform identifier with its registration rather than with the visual app title. Android applicationId is the Play identity and cannot change after upload. The supplied change to applicationId and namespace also requires the MainActivity package declaration and directory placement to match. iOS uses the Runner Bundle Identifier matching its registered App ID. Keep those platform fields separate in the verification table, then compare them with intended service and store registrations. A correct-looking display name does not establish any of these identity matches, and a successful earlier upload does not make Android identity freely changeable for a later update.",
103: "The Flutter version declaration provides values that must be verified after platform mapping. On Android, the visible versionName and increasing positive versionCode have different roles; a new label does not make a reused code valid. On iOS, inspect CFBundleShortVersionString and CFBundleVersion, then validate the bundle-ID, version-record, and build-string combination under Apple's upload rules. Build flags can apply the intended values, so check the resulting platform fields rather than assuming pubspec text is the only input. Keep the Android rule qualified to Android rather than turning it into a globally increasing-number rule for both stores.",
104: "The policy row is selected before the candidate comparison. Intended submission date and category determine what is effective and applicable. Google Play distinguishes new/update submissions from existing-app availability and includes platform exceptions and dated deadlines. Apple publishes dated SDK/toolchain requirements for uploads. Use those supplied authoritative items to compare target SDK or build SDK and Xcode version as appropriate. The installed toolchain and a successful local build are candidate observations, not policy approval. Mark unmet items explicitly without inventing an up-to-date minimum from memory, and retain the date/category qualifications when describing whether this candidate meets the check.",
109: "Map actual behavior into disclosures rather than mapping desired disclosures back into a fictional build. Include collection and sharing performed by third-party SDKs as well as application code. The policy covers the stated uses, sharing, retention, deletion, and consent withdrawal, while Data safety uses the inventory for its relevant collection/sharing/security/deletion declarations. Compare permission and telemetry implementation with those declarations, especially after a change. The exercise asks for fidelity to the shipped behavior, not new legal advice or invented SDK practices. Keep each supplied inventory item traceable to the applicable disclosure instead of assuming SDK data is outside the application's report.",
110: "Use the same release journey for the user-facing check and the reviewer-access check. The release build should work on its intended targets with live accessible backend services, and the reviewer should have working access to account-gated functionality. Then inspect Play's release review summary. Errors are resolved before publishing; warnings are addressed where possible, preserving that qualification. A successful startup, a nominal demo account, and an uploaded build are separate observations that do not replace completing the main flow. Record a concrete blocking failure when one exists rather than treating the presence of release artifacts as review readiness.",
}
